1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,114
Sweetheart, you're eighteen next spring,

2
00:00:03,114 --> 00:00:03,970
aren't you?

3
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:05,776
Your mother told me.

4
00:00:05,956 --> 00:00:07,151
Eighteen.

5
00:00:07,331 --> 00:00:09,958
Well, let me tell you what eighteen

6
00:00:09,958 --> 00:00:11,383
looked like for me.

7
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:13,699
It was nineteen sixty-two.

8
00:00:13,879 --> 00:00:17,377
I was finishing high school in Mansfield,

9
00:00:17,377 --> 00:00:17,804
Ohio.

10
00:00:17,984 --> 00:00:19,889
And that was the year a man

11
00:00:19,889 --> 00:00:22,147
flew around the whole world in a

12
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:24,123
tin can, and the year we all

13
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:26,381
went to bed one night in October

14
00:00:26,381 --> 00:00:27,933
not sure we'd wake up.

15
00:00:28,283 --> 00:00:30,307
I'm going to tell you all of

16
00:00:30,307 --> 00:00:30,524
it.

17
00:00:30,704 --> 00:00:32,149
What things cost,

18
00:00:32,149 --> 00:00:33,253
what we wore,

19
00:00:33,253 --> 00:00:35,207
what we were scared of,

20
00:00:35,207 --> 00:00:37,671
and what we did on a Saturday

21
00:00:37,671 --> 00:00:38,181
night.

22
00:00:38,361 --> 00:00:41,461
Because when people talk about the sixties

23
00:00:41,461 --> 00:00:44,561
now, they talk about hippies and protests.

24
00:00:44,741 --> 00:00:46,218
That came later,

25
00:00:46,218 --> 00:00:46,773
honey.

26
00:00:46,953 --> 00:00:49,853
In sixty-two we were still wearing white

27
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:51,086
gloves to church.

28
00:00:51,436 --> 00:00:52,812
My senior year,

29
00:00:52,812 --> 00:00:54,280
I got up at six.

30
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:56,610
I set my hair the night before

31
00:00:56,610 --> 00:00:59,119
on pink sponge rollers and slept on

32
00:00:59,119 --> 00:01:01,843
them, which is about as comfortable as

33
00:01:01,843 --> 00:01:03,922
sleeping on a bag of walnuts.

34
00:01:04,102 --> 00:01:05,894
Then a whole can of Aqua Net

35
00:01:05,894 --> 00:01:06,598
to hold it.

36
00:01:06,778 --> 00:01:08,818
We wore skirts to school,

37
00:01:08,818 --> 00:01:09,634
every day.

38
00:01:09,814 --> 00:01:12,732
Girls weren't allowed to wear pants to

39
00:01:12,732 --> 00:01:13,193
class.

40
00:01:13,373 --> 00:01:14,916
Not even in January,

41
00:01:14,916 --> 00:01:17,309
with the snow up to your knees.

42
00:01:17,659 --> 00:01:19,823
We had a dress code you wouldn't

43
00:01:19,823 --> 00:01:20,364
believe.

44
00:01:20,544 --> 00:01:22,697
Skirts had to touch the floor when

45
00:01:22,697 --> 00:01:23,647
you knelt down,

46
00:01:23,647 --> 00:01:26,181
and the vice principal really would make

47
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:28,334
you kneel in the hallway to check.

48
00:01:28,514 --> 00:01:31,113
Boys couldn't let their hair touch their

49
00:01:31,113 --> 00:01:31,567
collar.

50
00:01:31,747 --> 00:01:33,838
A boy in my class got sent

51
00:01:33,838 --> 00:01:36,089
home for wearing blue jeans.

52
00:01:36,269 --> 00:01:37,686
Blue jeans!

53
00:01:37,866 --> 00:01:40,117
That was for working on the farm,

54
00:01:40,117 --> 00:01:41,140
not for school.

55
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:44,172
After school I worked at the Woolworth's

56
00:01:44,172 --> 00:01:46,921
downtown, three afternoons a week and all

57
00:01:46,921 --> 00:01:49,268
day Saturday, for a dollar an hour.

58
00:01:49,448 --> 00:01:51,559
I gave half to my mother for

59
00:01:51,559 --> 00:01:54,499
the house, because that's what you did.

60
00:01:54,679 --> 00:01:56,932
The other half went into a coffee

61
00:01:56,932 --> 00:01:58,162
can on my dresser,

62
00:01:58,162 --> 00:02:00,074
and I'll tell you what I was

63
00:02:00,074 --> 00:02:01,645
saving for in a minute.

64
00:02:01,995 --> 00:02:04,535
Now, the twentieth of February,

65
00:02:04,535 --> 00:02:06,093
nineteen sixty-two.

66
00:02:06,273 --> 00:02:09,212
That morning they rolled a television set

67
00:02:09,212 --> 00:02:11,148
into our school auditorium,

68
00:02:11,148 --> 00:02:12,582
a big one on a cart,

69
00:02:12,582 --> 00:02:14,804
and the whole school sat on the

70
00:02:14,804 --> 00:02:16,095
floor and watched.

71
00:02:16,275 --> 00:02:19,433
John Glenn, a Marine from New Concord,

72
00:02:19,433 --> 00:02:22,176
Ohio, not even two hours from our

73
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:24,752
house, was going up in a rocket

74
00:02:24,752 --> 00:02:26,747
called Friendship Seven.

75
00:02:27,097 --> 00:02:29,494
The Russians had already put a man

76
00:02:29,494 --> 00:02:30,833
up the year before,

77
00:02:30,833 --> 00:02:33,090
and we were all a little ashamed

78
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:33,865
about that.

79
00:02:34,045 --> 00:02:36,282
So when that rocket went up off

80
00:02:36,282 --> 00:02:37,653
the pad in Florida,

81
00:02:37,653 --> 00:02:40,611
the whole auditorium screamed like it was

82
00:02:40,611 --> 00:02:41,766
a football game.

83
00:02:41,946 --> 00:02:44,460
He went around the earth three times

84
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:46,137
in less than five hours.

85
00:02:46,317 --> 00:02:48,163
Our teacher, Mr.

86
00:02:48,343 --> 00:02:49,899
Hoover, cried.

87
00:02:50,079 --> 00:02:51,333
A grown man,

88
00:02:51,333 --> 00:02:53,631
our chemistry teacher,

89
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:54,258
cried.

90
00:02:54,609 --> 00:02:56,315
And then there was a problem.

91
00:02:56,495 --> 00:02:58,784
A light came on saying the heat

92
00:02:58,784 --> 00:03:00,408
shield might be loose.

93
00:03:00,588 --> 00:03:01,849
If it came off,

94
00:03:01,849 --> 00:03:04,373
he'd burn up coming back down.

95
00:03:04,553 --> 00:03:06,622
For a few minutes nobody in the

96
00:03:06,622 --> 00:03:07,757
room said a word.

97
00:03:07,937 --> 00:03:10,419
Then he splashed down in the ocean

98
00:03:10,419 --> 00:03:12,098
and they picked him up,

99
00:03:12,098 --> 00:03:13,266
and he was fine.

100
00:03:13,446 --> 00:03:15,594
Ohio went crazy.

101
00:03:15,774 --> 00:03:18,167
He got a ticker-tape parade in New

102
00:03:18,167 --> 00:03:20,278
York with four million people.

103
00:03:20,629 --> 00:03:23,141
Let me tell you what things cost,

104
00:03:23,141 --> 00:03:25,273
honey, because you'll laugh.

105
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:28,448
Gas was about thirty-one cents a gallon.

106
00:03:28,628 --> 00:03:31,484
A loaf of bread was twenty cents.

107
00:03:31,664 --> 00:03:33,450
A stamp was four cents,

108
00:03:33,450 --> 00:03:35,702
until January of sixty-three,

109
00:03:35,702 --> 00:03:37,799
when it went up to five and

110
00:03:37,799 --> 00:03:41,138
everybody complained like it was a robbery.

111
00:03:41,318 --> 00:03:44,276
A hamburger at the drive-in was fifteen

112
00:03:44,276 --> 00:03:44,731
cents.

113
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:47,381
A brand new Chevrolet was about two

114
00:03:47,381 --> 00:03:49,353
thousand five hundred dollars.

115
00:03:49,533 --> 00:03:52,447
A nice house was around twelve thousand.

116
00:03:52,627 --> 00:03:55,153
My father made about ninety dollars a

117
00:03:55,153 --> 00:03:57,270
week at the Westinghouse plant,

118
00:03:57,270 --> 00:03:58,908
and we were comfortable.

119
00:03:59,088 --> 00:04:02,002
The minimum wage was a dollar fifteen.

120
00:04:02,182 --> 00:04:04,405
You could live on one paycheck,

121
00:04:04,405 --> 00:04:05,481
a whole family,

122
00:04:05,481 --> 00:04:07,058
with a mother at home.

123
00:04:07,238 --> 00:04:09,407
I know that sounds like a fairy

124
00:04:09,407 --> 00:04:10,036
tale now.

125
00:04:10,387 --> 00:04:12,791
And here's what was in my coffee

126
00:04:12,791 --> 00:04:13,092
can.

127
00:04:13,272 --> 00:04:15,454
Seventy-two dollars by May.

128
00:04:15,634 --> 00:04:18,571
I was saving for my graduation dress

129
00:04:18,571 --> 00:04:21,426
and a pair of real nylon stockings,

130
00:04:21,426 --> 00:04:23,465
and if anything was left,

131
00:04:23,465 --> 00:04:25,015
a transistor radio.

132
00:04:25,195 --> 00:04:27,398
A little one you could carry in

133
00:04:27,398 --> 00:04:29,317
your pocket, made in Japan.

134
00:04:29,497 --> 00:04:31,830
It cost about twenty dollars.

135
00:04:32,010 --> 00:04:33,285
With that radio,

136
00:04:33,285 --> 00:04:35,515
I could listen to WHK out of

137
00:04:35,515 --> 00:04:38,303
Cleveland under my covers at night.

138
00:04:38,653 --> 00:04:40,382
Oh, the music.

139
00:04:40,562 --> 00:04:43,291
That year everybody was doing the Twist.

140
00:04:43,471 --> 00:04:46,285
Chubby Checker's song had come back to

141
00:04:46,285 --> 00:04:47,914
number one in January,

142
00:04:47,914 --> 00:04:50,802
and every grownup in America was trying

143
00:04:50,802 --> 00:04:52,209
it, even my mother,

144
00:04:52,209 --> 00:04:53,320
in the kitchen,

145
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:55,986
when she thought nobody was looking.

146
00:04:56,166 --> 00:04:58,232
Then there was Ray Charles,

147
00:04:58,232 --> 00:05:00,067
I Can't Stop Loving You.

148
00:05:00,247 --> 00:05:02,137
And the Four Seasons,

149
00:05:02,137 --> 00:05:02,767
Sherry.

150
00:05:02,947 --> 00:05:04,910
The boys all tried to sing it

151
00:05:04,910 --> 00:05:06,534
high like Frankie Valli.

152
00:05:06,714 --> 00:05:07,620
They couldn't.

153
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:10,396
Television changed that year too.

154
00:05:10,576 --> 00:05:12,904
In July they sent the first live

155
00:05:12,904 --> 00:05:16,178
television picture across the Atlantic Ocean,

156
00:05:16,178 --> 00:05:18,506
from a satellite called Telstar.

157
00:05:18,686 --> 00:05:21,331
We saw France and England live,

158
00:05:21,331 --> 00:05:23,208
in our own front room,

159
00:05:23,208 --> 00:05:24,829
and my father said,

160
00:05:24,829 --> 00:05:27,474
well, now I've seen everything.

161
00:05:27,654 --> 00:05:29,864
And in October a young man from

162
00:05:29,864 --> 00:05:32,858
Nebraska named Johnny Carson took over The

163
00:05:32,858 --> 00:05:33,784
Tonight Show.

164
00:05:33,964 --> 00:05:36,437
My parents were never up that late.

165
00:05:36,617 --> 00:05:37,627
I was.

166
00:05:37,978 --> 00:05:40,645
On Saturday nights there was a dance

167
00:05:40,645 --> 00:05:41,534
at the YMCA,

168
00:05:41,534 --> 00:05:43,016
a quarter to get in.

169
00:05:43,196 --> 00:05:44,404
A record player,

170
00:05:44,404 --> 00:05:45,385
a punch bowl,

171
00:05:45,385 --> 00:05:48,101
and a chaperone standing by the wall

172
00:05:48,101 --> 00:05:50,969
making sure everyone left room for the

173
00:05:50,969 --> 00:05:52,856
Holy Spirit between them.

174
00:05:53,036 --> 00:05:54,727
That's what they called it,

175
00:05:54,727 --> 00:05:55,102
honey.

176
00:05:55,282 --> 00:05:56,710
The Holy Spirit.

177
00:05:56,890 --> 00:05:58,133
Six inches.

178
00:05:58,313 --> 00:06:00,344
The chaperone had a ruler.

179
00:06:00,695 --> 00:06:03,115
That's where I really got to know

180
00:06:03,115 --> 00:06:05,536
your grandfather, Tommy Callahan.

181
00:06:05,716 --> 00:06:08,062
He'd taught me to drive two summers

182
00:06:08,062 --> 00:06:10,005
before in his father's truck,

183
00:06:10,005 --> 00:06:12,217
and now he was twenty and working

184
00:06:12,217 --> 00:06:13,088
at the plant.

185
00:06:13,268 --> 00:06:15,786
He wore his hair slicked back and

186
00:06:15,786 --> 00:06:17,541
smelled like Old Spice.

187
00:06:17,721 --> 00:06:19,893
He asked me to dance to a

188
00:06:19,893 --> 00:06:22,848
slow song, and he left exactly six

189
00:06:22,848 --> 00:06:23,456
inches.

190
00:06:23,636 --> 00:06:25,447
I was so disappointed.

191
00:06:25,798 --> 00:06:27,490
We graduated in June,

192
00:06:27,490 --> 00:06:28,859
in the gymnasium,

193
00:06:28,859 --> 00:06:29,826
in the heat.

194
00:06:30,006 --> 00:06:32,827
Four hundred of us in maroon gowns.

195
00:06:33,007 --> 00:06:35,492
My father wore his only suit.

196
00:06:35,672 --> 00:06:37,575
My mother wore a hat with a

197
00:06:37,575 --> 00:06:38,702
little net veil.

198
00:06:38,882 --> 00:06:41,471
And Grandma Pearl sat in the front

199
00:06:41,471 --> 00:06:44,137
row and clapped louder than anyone,

200
00:06:44,137 --> 00:06:46,954
because she had left school at twelve

201
00:06:46,954 --> 00:06:48,553
to work in a laundry.

202
00:06:48,733 --> 00:06:50,636
I was the first girl in our

203
00:06:50,636 --> 00:06:52,681
family to finish high school.

204
00:06:53,031 --> 00:06:55,852
Now, here's something you won't believe.

205
00:06:56,032 --> 00:06:57,039
In my class,

206
00:06:57,039 --> 00:06:59,473
most of the girls I knew were

207
00:06:59,473 --> 00:07:02,998
already engaged, or would be by Christmas.

208
00:07:03,178 --> 00:07:05,535
Eighteen, nineteen.

209
00:07:05,715 --> 00:07:06,992
That was normal.

210
00:07:07,172 --> 00:07:09,955
If a girl wasn't married by twenty-two

211
00:07:09,955 --> 00:07:13,325
or twenty-three, the aunts started whispering.

212
00:07:13,505 --> 00:07:15,198
Only a few of us went to

213
00:07:15,198 --> 00:07:18,091
college, and the counselor told the girls

214
00:07:18,091 --> 00:07:19,220
to be a teacher,

215
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:20,842
a nurse or a secretary.

216
00:07:21,022 --> 00:07:22,857
Those were the choices.

217
00:07:23,207 --> 00:07:25,959
That summer of sixty-two was a hot

218
00:07:25,959 --> 00:07:26,283
one.

219
00:07:26,463 --> 00:07:29,156
On Sundays the whole family drove out

220
00:07:29,156 --> 00:07:31,631
to Charles Mill Lake with a cooler

221
00:07:31,631 --> 00:07:34,834
full of baloney sandwiches and a watermelon.

222
00:07:35,014 --> 00:07:37,336
No sunscreen, honey.

223
00:07:37,516 --> 00:07:39,735
We rubbed baby oil on our arms

224
00:07:39,735 --> 00:07:41,214
to get brown faster,

225
00:07:41,214 --> 00:07:43,432
and some girls used a sheet of

226
00:07:43,432 --> 00:07:46,316
tinfoil under their chin like a mirror.

227
00:07:46,496 --> 00:07:49,167
I peeled like an onion every August.

228
00:07:49,347 --> 00:07:51,216
Every single August.

229
00:07:51,566 --> 00:07:52,832
And in August,

230
00:07:52,832 --> 00:07:56,361
the radio said Marilyn Monroe had died,

231
00:07:56,361 --> 00:07:57,719
in Los Angeles.

232
00:07:57,899 --> 00:07:59,838
She was only thirty-six.

233
00:08:00,018 --> 00:08:02,664
The girls at the Woolworth's counter talked

234
00:08:02,664 --> 00:08:04,511
about nothing else for a week.

235
00:08:04,691 --> 00:08:06,662
I had a picture of her from

236
00:08:06,662 --> 00:08:09,509
a magazine taped inside my closet door.

237
00:08:09,689 --> 00:08:11,634
My mother made me take it down

238
00:08:11,634 --> 00:08:12,800
when I was twelve.

239
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:14,827
I put it back up when I

240
00:08:14,827 --> 00:08:15,790
was sixteen.

241
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:18,970
That summer two new kinds of stores

242
00:08:18,970 --> 00:08:22,204
opened, and nobody in Mansfield paid any

243
00:08:22,204 --> 00:08:23,013
attention.

244
00:08:23,193 --> 00:08:25,962
In Arkansas a man named Sam Walton

245
00:08:25,962 --> 00:08:28,080
opened his first Wal-Mart.

246
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:29,908
And outside Detroit,

247
00:08:29,908 --> 00:08:33,369
the Kresge company opened its first Kmart.

248
00:08:33,549 --> 00:08:36,023
We all thought downtown would be there

249
00:08:36,023 --> 00:08:36,544
forever.

250
00:08:36,724 --> 00:08:38,350
It wasn't, honey.

251
00:08:38,530 --> 00:08:40,468
But that's another story.

252
00:08:40,819 --> 00:08:42,676
And then came October.

253
00:08:42,856 --> 00:08:44,944
On Monday, the twenty-second,

254
00:08:44,944 --> 00:08:47,969
President Kennedy came on every channel at

255
00:08:47,969 --> 00:08:49,625
seven o'clock at night.

256
00:08:49,805 --> 00:08:51,875
We all sat in front of our

257
00:08:51,875 --> 00:08:52,591
Motorola.

258
00:08:52,771 --> 00:08:55,697
He said the Russians had put nuclear

259
00:08:55,697 --> 00:08:57,078
missiles in Cuba,

260
00:08:57,078 --> 00:08:59,191
ninety miles from Florida.

261
00:08:59,371 --> 00:09:02,013
And that any missile fired from Cuba

262
00:09:02,013 --> 00:09:04,362
would be treated as an attack by

263
00:09:04,362 --> 00:09:07,150
the Soviet Union on the United States.

264
00:09:07,501 --> 00:09:09,817
I want you to understand what that

265
00:09:09,817 --> 00:09:10,635
meant to us.

266
00:09:10,815 --> 00:09:12,909
We had grown up with the duck

267
00:09:12,909 --> 00:09:14,136
and cover drills.

268
00:09:14,316 --> 00:09:16,329
Since I was in third grade,

269
00:09:16,329 --> 00:09:18,418
the teacher would yell drop,

270
00:09:18,418 --> 00:09:21,177
and we'd crawl under our wooden desks

271
00:09:21,177 --> 00:09:23,639
and put our hands over our necks.

272
00:09:23,819 --> 00:09:25,932
As if a desk would stop an

273
00:09:25,932 --> 00:09:26,907
atomic bomb.

274
00:09:27,087 --> 00:09:28,550
But we believed it.

275
00:09:28,730 --> 00:09:29,925
We were children.

276
00:09:30,276 --> 00:09:32,030
At school the next day,

277
00:09:32,030 --> 00:09:34,699
nobody could think about chemistry.

278
00:09:34,879 --> 00:09:37,667
The teachers had us practice the drill

279
00:09:37,667 --> 00:09:39,721
again, all of us lined up in

280
00:09:39,721 --> 00:09:41,262
the basement hallway,

281
00:09:41,262 --> 00:09:44,123
sitting against the wall with our heads

282
00:09:44,123 --> 00:09:45,444
between our knees.

283
00:09:45,624 --> 00:09:48,454
A girl named Carol started crying and

284
00:09:48,454 --> 00:09:49,525
couldn't stop.

285
00:09:49,705 --> 00:09:52,661
And the principal came on the loudspeaker

286
00:09:52,661 --> 00:09:54,175
and asked us to pray.

287
00:09:54,355 --> 00:09:55,841
In a public school.

288
00:09:56,021 --> 00:09:58,540
Nobody thought twice about it then.

289
00:09:58,891 --> 00:10:01,731
That week, people in Mansfield bought out

290
00:10:01,731 --> 00:10:03,047
the grocery stores.

291
00:10:03,227 --> 00:10:05,741
Canned soup, bottled water,

292
00:10:05,741 --> 00:10:07,697
flashlight batteries.

293
00:10:07,877 --> 00:10:09,906
A man two streets over had a

294
00:10:09,906 --> 00:10:12,008
bomb shelter in his backyard,

295
00:10:12,008 --> 00:10:14,835
and everybody suddenly wanted to be his

296
00:10:14,835 --> 00:10:15,342
friend.

297
00:10:15,522 --> 00:10:17,825
My father didn't say much,

298
00:10:17,825 --> 00:10:18,622
as usual.

299
00:10:18,802 --> 00:10:21,487
He just filled the bathtub with water

300
00:10:21,487 --> 00:10:23,156
every night before bed.

301
00:10:23,336 --> 00:10:24,218
Every night.

302
00:10:24,398 --> 00:10:27,150
That frightened me more than the television.

303
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:28,942
At the Woolworth's,

304
00:10:28,942 --> 00:10:31,598
nobody bought anything but candles.

305
00:10:31,778 --> 00:10:34,468
Tommy came by the counter on Thursday

306
00:10:34,468 --> 00:10:35,704
and said, Ruthie,

307
00:10:35,704 --> 00:10:37,522
if this is the end of the

308
00:10:37,522 --> 00:10:39,922
world, I don't want to go without

309
00:10:39,922 --> 00:10:41,449
asking you something.

310
00:10:41,629 --> 00:10:42,463
And I said,

311
00:10:42,463 --> 00:10:45,270
Tommy Callahan, don't you dare ask me

312
00:10:45,270 --> 00:10:48,305
anything at a Woolworth's lunch counter.

313
00:10:48,485 --> 00:10:49,460
So he didn't.

314
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:50,789
Not then.

315
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:53,261
On Sunday, the twenty-eighth,

316
00:10:53,261 --> 00:10:55,821
it was on the radio that Khrushchev

317
00:10:55,821 --> 00:10:58,454
had agreed to take the missiles out.

318
00:10:58,634 --> 00:11:00,507
The church bells rang,

319
00:11:00,507 --> 00:11:01,443
I remember.

320
00:11:01,623 --> 00:11:04,247
My mother cried into her dish towel.

321
00:11:04,427 --> 00:11:07,028
And my father drained the bathtub.

322
00:11:07,208 --> 00:11:09,251
Thirteen days, honey.

323
00:11:09,431 --> 00:11:11,925
For thirteen days the whole world held

324
00:11:11,925 --> 00:11:14,155
its breath, and I was eighteen and

325
00:11:14,155 --> 00:11:16,583
thought I'd never get to be nineteen.

326
00:11:16,933 --> 00:11:19,046
That Christmas felt different.

327
00:11:19,226 --> 00:11:21,699
Everyone held on a little tighter.

328
00:11:21,879 --> 00:11:24,355
We went to church on Christmas Eve,

329
00:11:24,355 --> 00:11:26,619
and the place was so full people

330
00:11:26,619 --> 00:11:27,892
stood in the back.

331
00:11:28,072 --> 00:11:29,457
My brother Danny,

332
00:11:29,457 --> 00:11:30,761
who was fifteen,

333
00:11:30,761 --> 00:11:33,286
sang in the choir and his voice

334
00:11:33,286 --> 00:11:35,241
cracked on O Holy Night,

335
00:11:35,241 --> 00:11:36,789
and nobody laughed,

336
00:11:36,789 --> 00:11:37,767
not even me.

337
00:11:38,117 --> 00:11:40,054
And on Christmas night,

338
00:11:40,054 --> 00:11:42,076
Tommy came over for pie.

339
00:11:42,256 --> 00:11:44,056
And on our front porch,

340
00:11:44,056 --> 00:11:45,309
under the light,

341
00:11:45,309 --> 00:11:46,248
in the cold,

342
00:11:46,248 --> 00:11:49,224
with my father pretending not to watch

343
00:11:49,224 --> 00:11:50,476
from the window,

344
00:11:50,476 --> 00:11:51,416
he asked me.

345
00:11:51,596 --> 00:11:53,419
Not at a lunch counter.

346
00:11:53,599 --> 00:11:55,634
He had a ring that cost him

347
00:11:55,634 --> 00:11:56,841
four weeks' pay,

348
00:11:56,841 --> 00:11:59,329
and I said yes before he finished

349
00:11:59,329 --> 00:12:00,309
the question.

350
00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:02,506
So that was eighteen.

351
00:12:02,686 --> 00:12:04,613
No phone in my pocket.

352
00:12:04,793 --> 00:12:06,198
No computer.

353
00:12:06,378 --> 00:12:09,536
A transistor radio and a coffee can.

354
00:12:09,716 --> 00:12:11,460
We were scared of the bomb,

355
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:13,268
and we did the Twist anyway.

356
00:12:13,448 --> 00:12:15,792
We thought the world could end on

357
00:12:15,792 --> 00:12:17,923
a Tuesday, and we went to work

358
00:12:17,923 --> 00:12:18,847
on Wednesday.

359
00:12:19,027 --> 00:12:21,563
I'm not telling you it was better,

360
00:12:21,563 --> 00:12:22,011
honey.

361
00:12:22,191 --> 00:12:23,816
Some of it was terrible.

362
00:12:23,996 --> 00:12:26,179
But we knew how to keep going.

363
00:12:26,529 --> 00:12:28,900
Now, tell me in the comments,

364
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,371
if you were there.

365
00:12:30,551 --> 00:12:33,591
Where were you during those thirteen days

366
00:12:33,591 --> 00:12:34,406
in October?

367
00:12:34,586 --> 00:12:36,606
And what did your father do?

368
00:12:36,786 --> 00:12:38,948
I'd love to know if he filled

369
00:12:38,948 --> 00:12:40,141
the bathtub too.

370
00:12:40,321 --> 00:12:42,016
Goodnight, sweetheart.

371
00:12:42,196 --> 00:12:45,051
Grandma Ruth will be right here with

372
00:12:45,051 --> 00:12:46,875
another story tomorrow.
